ck_estimator`.\n```\n662 | def check_estimator(\n663 |     estimator=None,\n664 |     generate_only=False,\n665 |     *,\n666 |     legacy: bool = True,\n667 |     expected_failed_checks: dict[str, str] | None = None,\n668 |     on_skip: Literal[\"warn\"] | None = \"warn\",\n669 |     on_fail: Literal[\"raise\", \"warn\"] | None = \"raise\",\n670 |     callback: Callable | None = None,\n671 | ):\n672 |     \"\"\"Check if estimator adheres to scikit-learn conventions.\n673 | \n674 |     This function will run an extensive test-suite for input validation,\n675 |     shapes, etc, making sure that the estimator complies with `scikit-learn`\n676 |     conventions as detailed in :ref:`rolling_your_own_estimator`.\n677 |     Additional tests for classifiers, regressors, clustering or transformers\n678 |     will be run if the Estimator class inherits from the corresponding mixin\n679 |     from sklearn.base.\n680 | \n681 |     scikit-learn also provides a pytest specific decorator,\n682 |     :func:`~sklearn.utils.estimator_checks.parametrize_with_checks`, making it\n683 |     easier to test multiple estimators.\n684 | \n685 |     Checks are categorised into the following groups:\n686 | \n687 |     - API checks: a set of checks to ensure API compatibility with scikit-learn.\n688 |       Refer to https://scikit-learn.org/dev/developers/develop.html a requirement of\n689 |       scikit-learn estimators.\n690 |     - legacy: a set of checks which gradually will be grouped into other categories.\n691 | \n692 |     Parameters\n693 |     ----------\n694 |     estimator : estimator object\n695 |         Estimator instance to check.\n696 | \n697 |     generate_only : bool, default=False\n698 |         When `False`, checks are evaluated when `check_estimator` is called.\n699 |         When `True`, `check_estimator` returns a generator that yields\n700 |         (estimator, check) tuples. The check is run by calling\n701 |         `check(estimator)`.\n702 | \n703 |         .. versionadded:: 0.22\n704 | \n705 |         .. deprecated:: 1.6\n706 |             `generate_only` will be removed in 1.8. Use\n707 |             :func:`~sklearn.utils.estimator_checks.estimator_checks_generator` instead.\n708 | \n709 |     legacy : bool, default=True\n710 |         Whether to include legacy checks. Over time we remove checks from this category\n711 |         and move them into their specific category.\n712 | \n713 |         .. versionadded:: 1.6\n714 | \n715 |     expected_failed_checks : dict, default=None\n716 |         A dictionary of the form::\n717 | \n718 |             {\n719 |                 \"check_name\": \"this check is expected to fail because ...\",\n720 |             }\n721 | \n722 |         Where `\"check_name\"` is the name of the check, and `\"my reason\"` is why\n723 |         the check fails.\n724 | \n725 |         .. versionadded:: 1.6\n726 | \n727 |     on_skip : \"warn\", None, default=\"warn\"\n728 |         This parameter controls what happens when a check is skipped.\n729 | \n730 |         - \"warn\": A :class:`~sklearn.exceptions.SkipTestWarning` is logged\n731 |           and running tests continue.\n732 |         - None: No warning is logged and running tests continue.\n733 | \n734 |         .. versionadded:: 1.6\n735 | \n736 |     on_fail : {\"raise\", \"warn\"}, None, default=\"raise\"\n737 |         This parameter controls what happens when a check fails.\n738 | \n739 |         - \"raise\": The exception raised by the first failing check is raised and\n740 |           running tests are aborted. This does not included tests that are expected\n741 |           to fail.\n742 |         - \"warn\": A :class:`~sklearn.exceptions.EstimatorCheckFailedWarning` is logged\n743 |           and running tests continue.\n744 |         - None: No exception is raised and no warning is logged.\n745 | \n746 |         Note that if ``on_fail != \"raise\"``, no exception is raised, even if the checks\n747 |         fail. You'd need to inspect the return result of ``check_estimator`` to check\n748 |         if any checks failed.\n749 | \n750 |         .. versionadded:: 1.6\n751 | \n752 |     callback : callable, or None, default=None\n753 |         This callback will be called with the estimator and the check name,\n754 |         the exception (if any), the status of the check (xfail, failed, skipped,\n755 |         passed), and the reason for the expected failure if the check is\n756 |         expected to fail. The callable's signature needs to be::\n757 | \n758 |             def callback(\n759 |                 estimator,\n760 |                 check_name: str,\n761 |                 exception: Exception,\n762 |                 status: Literal[\"xfail\", \"failed\", \"skipped\", \"passed\"],\n763 |                 expected_to_fail: bool,\n764 |                 expected_to_fail_reason: str,\n765 |             )\n766 | \n767 |         ``callback`` cannot be provided together with ``on_fail=\"raise\"``.\n768 | \n769 |         .. versionadded:: 1.6\n770 | \n771 |     Returns\n772 |     -------\n773 |     test_results : list\n774 |         List of dictionaries with the results of the failing tests, of the form::\n775 | \n776 |             {\n777 |                 \"estimator\": estimator,\n778 |                 \"check_name\": check_name,\n779 |                 \"exception\": exception,\n780 |                 \"status\": status (one of \"xfail\", \"failed\", \"skipped\", \"passed\"),\n781 |                 \"expected_to_fail\": expected_to_fail,\n782 |                 \"expected_to_fail_reason\": expected_to_fail_reason,\n783 |             }\n784 | \n785 |     estimator_checks_generator : generator\n786 |         Generator that yields (estimator, check) tuples. Returned when\n787 |         `generate_only=True`.\n788 | \n789 |         ..\n790 |             TODO(1.8): remove return value\n791 | \n792 |         .. deprecated:: 1.6\n793 |             ``generate_only`` will be removed in 1.8. Use\n794 |             :func:`~sklearn.utils.estimator_checks.estimator_checks_generator` instead.\n795 | \n796 |     Raises\n797 |     ------\n798 |     Exception\n799 |         If ``on_fail=\"raise\"``, the exception raised by the first failing check is\n800 |         raised and running tests are aborted.\n801 | \n802 |         Note that if ``on_fail != \"raise\"``, no exception is raised, even if the checks\n803 |         fail. You'd need to inspect the return result of ``check_estimator`` to check\n804 |         if any checks failed.\n805 | \n806 |     See Also\n807 |     --------\n808 |     parametrize_with_checks : Pytest specific decorator for parametrizing estimator\n809 |         checks.\n810 |     estimator_checks_generator : Generator that yields (estimator, check) tuples.\n811 | \n812 |     Examples\n813 |     --------\n814 |     >>> from sklearn.utils.estimator_checks import check_estimator\n815 |     >>> from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n816 |     >>> check_estimator(LogisticRegression())\n817 |     [...]\n818 |     \"\"\"\n819 |     if isinstance(estimator, type):\n820 |         msg = (\n821 |             \"Passing a class was deprecated in version 0.23 \"\n822 |             \"and isn't supported anymore from 0.24.\"\n823 |             \"Please pass an instance instead.\"\n824 |         )\n825 |         raise TypeError(msg)\n826 | \n827 |     if on_fail == \"raise\" and callback is not None:\n828 |         raise ValueError(\"callback cannot be provided together with on_fail='raise'\")\n829 | \n830 |     name = type(estimator).__name__\n831 | \n832 |     # TODO(1.8): remove generate_only\n833 |     if generate_only:\n834 |         warnings.warn(\n835 |             \"`generate_only` is deprecated in 1.6 and will be removed in 1.8. \"\n836 |             \"Use :func:`~sklearn.utils.estimator_checks.estimator_checks` instead.\",\n837 |             FutureWarning,\n838 |         )\n839 |         return estimator_checks_generator(\n840 |             estimator, legacy=legacy, expected_failed_checks=None, mark=\"skip\"\n841 |         )\n842 | \n843 |     test_results = []\n844 | \n845 |     for estimator, check in estimator_checks_generator(\n846 |         estimator,\n847 |         legacy=legacy,\n848 |         expected_failed_checks=expected_failed_checks,\n849 |         # Not marking tests to be skipped here, we run and simulate an xfail behavior\n850 |         mark=None,\n851 |     ):\n852 |         test_can_fail, reason = _should_be_skipped_or_marked(\n853 |             estimator, check, expected_failed_checks\n854 |         )\n855 |         try:\n856 |             check(estimator)\n857 |         except SkipTest as e:\n858 |             # We get here if the test raises SkipTest, which is expected in cases where\n859 |             # the check cannot run for instance if a required dependency is not\n860 |             # installed.\n861 |             check_result = {\n862 |                 \"estimator\": estimator,\n863 |                 \"check_name\": _check_name(check),\n864 |                 \"exception\": e,\n865 |                 \"status\": \"skipped\",\n866 |                 \"expected_to_fail\": test_can_fail,\n867 |                 \"expected_to_fail_reason\": reason,\n868 |             }\n869 |             if on_skip == \"warn\":\n870 |                 warnings.warn(\n871 |                     f\"Skipping check {_check_name(check)} for {name} because it raised \"\n872 |                     f\"{type(e).__name__}: {e}\",\n873 |                     SkipTestWarning,\n874 |                 )\n875 |         except Exception as e:\n876 |             if on_fail == \"raise\" and not test_can_fail:\n877 |                 raise\n878 | \n879 |             check_result = {\n880 |                 \"estimator\": estimator,\n881 |                 \"check_name\": _check_name(check),\n882 |                 \"exception\": e,\n883 |                 \"expected_to_fail\": test_can_fail,\n884 |                 \"expected_to_fail_reason\": reason,\n885 |             }\n886 | \n887 |             if test_can_fail:\n888 |                 # This check failed, but could be expected to fail, therefore we mark it\n889 |                 # as xfail.\n890 |                 check_result[\"status\"] = \"xfail\"\n891 |             else:\n892 |                 failed = True\n893 |                 check_result[\"status\"] = \"failed\"\n894 | \n895 |             if on_fail == \"warn\":\n896 |                 warning = EstimatorCheckFailedWarning(**check_result)\n897 |                 warnings.warn(warning)\n898 |         else:\n899 |             check_result = {\n900 |                 \"estimator\": estimator,\n901 |                 \"check_name\": _check_name(check),\n902 |                 \"exception\": None,\n903 |                 \"status\": \"passed\",\n904 |                 \"expected_to_fail\": test_can_fail,\n905 |                 \"expected_to_fail_reason\": reason,\n906 |             }\n907 | \n908 |         test_results.append(check_result)\n909 | \n910 |         if callback:\n911 |             callback(**check_result)\n912 | \n913 |     return test_results\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"BaseEstimator\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `sklearn/base.py:BaseEstimator`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `BaseEstimator`.\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass BaseEstimator(_HTMLDocumentationLinkMixin, _MetadataRequester):\n\n    @classmethod\n    def _get_param_names(cls):\n...\n    def get_params(self, deep=True):\n...\n    def set_params(self, **params):\n...\n    def __sklearn_clone__(self):\n...\n    def __repr__(self, N_CHAR_MAX=700):\n...\n    def __getstate__(self):\n...\n    def __setstate__(self, state):\n...\n    def __sklearn_tags__(self):\n...\n    def _validate_params(self):\n...\n    @property\n    def _repr_html_(self):\n...\n    def _repr_html_inner(self):\n...\n    def _repr_mimebundle_(self, **kwargs):\n...\n    # TODO(1.7): Remove t