e content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  4 | import functools\n  5 | import warnings\n  6 | from inspect import signature\n  7 | \n  8 | __all__ = [\"deprecated\"]\n  9 | \n 10 | \n 11 | class deprecated:\n 12 |     \"\"\"Decorator to mark a function or class as deprecated.\n 13 | \n 14 |     Issue a warning when the function is called/the class is instantiated and\n 15 |     adds a warning to the docstring.\n 16 | \n 17 |     The optional extra argument will be appended to the deprecation message\n 18 |     and the docstring. Note: to use this with the default value for extra, put\n 19 |     in an empty of parentheses:\n 20 | \n 21 |     Examples\n 22 |     --------\n 23 |     >>> from sklearn.utils import deprecated\n 24 |     >>> deprecated()\n 25 |     <sklearn.utils.deprecation.deprecated object at ...>\n 26 |     >>> @deprecated()\n 27 |     ... def some_function(): pass\n 28 | \n 29 |     Parameters\n 30 |     ----------\n 31 |     extra : str, default=''\n 32 |           To be added to the deprecation messages.\n 33 |     \"\"\"\n 34 | \n 35 |     # Adapted from https://wiki.python.org/moin/PythonDecoratorLibrary,\n 36 |     # but with many changes.\n 37 | \n 38 |     def __init__(self, extra=\"\"):\n 39 |         self.extra = extra\n```\nHint: \nThe code for class `sklearn/utils/deprecation.py:deprecated` is incomplete; search `sklearn/utils/deprecation.py:deprecated` for the full content if needed.\n"}, {"content": "```\nsklearn/utils/_tags.py\nfunction: get_tags\n\nsklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py\nfunction: check_estimator_tags_renamed\n\nsklearn/base.py\nclass: BaseEstimator\nfunction: __sklearn_tags__\n\nsklearn/utils/deprecation.py\nclass: deprecated\nfunction: _decorate_fun\nfunction: _decorate_property\nfunction: _deprecate_force_all_finite\n\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py\nfunction: fit_transform\n```\n<finish></finish>", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "explore_tree_structure", "description": "\nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"start_entities": {"description": "List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).", "type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "direction": {"description": "Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.", "type": "string", "enum": ["upstream", "downstream", "both"], "default": "downstream"}, "traversal_depth": {"description": "Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).", "type": "integer", "default": 2}, "entity_type_filter": {"description": "List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}, "dependency_type_filter": {"description": "List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}}, "required": ["start_entities"]}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 64709, "completion_tokens": 315}, "time": 42.19474673271179}]}}
{"instance_id": "scikit-learn__scikit-learn-14800", "found_files": [["sklearn/datasets/_openml.py"]], "found_modules": [["sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_load_arff_response", "sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_download_data_to_bunch", "sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_description_by_id", "sklearn/ensemble/_hist_gradient_boosting/loss.py:LeastAbsoluteDeviation", "sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_convert_arff_data", "sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_convert_arff_data_dataframe"]], "found_entities": [["sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_load_arff_response", "sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_download_data_to_bunch", "sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_description_by_id", "sklearn/ensemble/_hist_gradient_boosting/loss.py:LeastAbsoluteDeviation.inverse_link_function", "sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_convert_arff_data", "sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_convert_arff_data_dataframe"]], "raw_output_loc": ["Based on the search results, we need to focus on the `_load_arff_response` function in `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py` to add the MD5 checksum validation logic. This function handles the downloading and parsing of the ARFF data.\n\nWe also need to ensure that the `_get_data_description_by_id` function is correctly retrieving the `md5_checksum` from the `data_description` dictionary.\n\nAdditionally, we should check the `_download_data_to_bunch` function to understand where the ARFF file is being saved locally and how the data is being processed.\n\nLet's also explore the `_convert_arff_data` and `_convert_arff_data_dataframe` functions to see if any modifications are needed to handle the checksum validation.\n\nHere are the relevant locations for implementing the feature:\n\n```\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 491\nfunction: _load_arff_response\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 509\nfunction: _download_data_to_bunch\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 430\nfunction: _get_data_description_by_id\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 233\nfunction: _convert_arff_data\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 310\nfunction: _convert_arff_data_dataframe\n```"], "meta_data": {"repo": "scikit-learn/scikit-learn", "base_commit": "acb8ac5145cfd88fdbd2d381b34883b2c212c8c5", "problem_statement": "Validate MD5 checksum of downloaded ARFF in fetch_openml\n`fetch_openml` downloads the `data_set_description` from openml.org which includes an `md5_checksum` field. This should correspond to the MD5 checksum of the downloaded ARFF data. We should (optionally?) use `hashlib` to verify that the MD5 matches.\n", "patch": "diff --git a/doc/whats_new/v0.24.rst b/doc/whats_new/v0.24.rst\nindex a647de6dac628..ba137fb76698f 100644\n--- a/doc/whats_new/v0.24.rst\n+++ b/doc/whats_new/v0.24.rst\n@@ -224,6 +224,11 @@ Changelog\n   :meth:`tree.DecisionTreeRegressor.fit`, and has not effect.\n   :pr:`17614` by :user:`Juan Carlos Alfaro Jim\u00e9nez <alfaro96>`.\n \n+:mod:`sklearn.datasets`\n+.......................\n+- |Feature| :func:`datasets.fetch_openml` now validates md5checksum of arff\n+  files downloaded or cached to ensure data integrity.\n+  :pr:`14800` by :user:`Shashank Singh <shashanksingh28>` and `Joel Nothman`_.\n \n Code and Documentation Contributors\n -----------------------------------\ndiff --git a/sklearn/datasets/_openml.py b/sklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nindex 6b537c09c59ad..d2ce5d471f8f7 100644\n--- a/sklearn/datasets/_openml.py\n+++ b/sklearn/datasets/_openml.py\n@@ -2,6 +2,7 @@\n import json\n import os\n import shutil\n+import hashlib\n from os.path import join\n from warnings import warn\n from contextlib import closing\n@@ -492,7 +493,8 @@ def _load_arff_response(\n     url: str,\n     data_home: Optional[str],\n     return_type, encode_nominal: bool,\n-    parse_arff: Callable[[ArffContainerType], Tuple]\n+    parse_arff: Callable[[ArffContainerType], Tuple],\n+    md5_checksum: str\n ) -> Tuple:\n     \"\"\"Load arff data with url and parses arff response with parse_arff\"\"\"\n     response = _open_openml_url(url, data_home)\n@@ -500,10 +502,32 @@ def _load_arff_response(\n     with closing(response):\n         # Note that if the data is dense, no reading is done until the data\n         # generator is iterated.\n-        arff = _arff.load((line.decode('utf-8') for line in response),\n+        actual_md5_checksum = hashlib.md5()\n+\n+        def _stream_checksum_generator(response):\n+            for line in response:\n+                actual_md5_checksum.update(line)\n+                yield line.decode('utf-8')\n+\n+        stream = _stream_checksum_generator(response)\n+\n+        arff = _arff.load(stream,\n                           return_type=return_type,\n                           encode_nominal=encode_nominal)\n-        return parse_arff(arff)\n+\n+        parsed_arff = parse_arff(arff)\n+\n+        # consume remaining stream, if early exited\n+        for _ in stream:\n+            pass\n+\n+        if actual_md5_checksum.hexdigest() != md5_checksum:\n+            raise ValueError(\"md5 checksum of local file for \" + url +\n+                             \" does not match description. \"\n+                             \"Downloaded file could have been modified / \"\n+                             \"corrupted, clean cache and retry...\")\n+\n+        return parsed_arff\n \n \n def _download_data_to_bunch(\n@@ -515,7 +539,8 @@ def _download_data_to_bunch(\n     features_list: List,\n     data_columns: List[int],\n     target_columns: List,\n-    shape: Optional[Tuple[int, int]]\n+    shape: Optional[Tuple[int, int]],\n+    md5_checksum: str\n ):\n     \"\"\"Download OpenML ARFF and convert to Bunch of data\n     \"\"\"\n@@ -609,7 +634,8 @@ def postprocess(X, y, nominal_attributes):\n         _load_arff_response)(url, data_home,\n                              return_type=return_type,\n                              encode_nominal=not as_frame,\n-                             parse_arff=parse_arff)\n+                             parse_arff=parse_arff,\n+                             md5_checksum=md5_checksum)\n     X, y, frame, nominal_attributes = postprocess(*out)\n \n     return Bunch(data=X, target=y, frame=frame,\n@@ -883,7 +909,9 @@ def fetch_openml(\n                                     as_frame=as_frame,\n                                     features_list=features_list, shape=shape,\n                                     target_columns=target_columns,\n-                                    data_columns=data_columns)\n+                                    data_columns=data_columns,\n+                                    md5_checksum=data_description[\n+                                        \"md5_checksum\"])\n \n     if return_X_y:\n         return bunch.data, bunch.target\n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 85525, "completion_tokens": 829}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\nexplore_graph_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 1, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Args:\n    start_entities:\n    direction:\n    traversal_depth:\n    entity_type_filter:\n    dependency_type_filter:\n    Returns:\n\nexplore_tree_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 2, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Analyzes and displays the dependency structure around specified entities in a code graph.\n    This function searches and presents relationships and dependencies for the specified entities (such as classes, functions, files, or directories) in a code graph.\n    It explores how the input entities relate to others, using defined types of dependencies, including 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes' and 'inherits'.\n    The search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n    Example Usage:\n    1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n    direction='downstream',\n    traversal_depth=2,\n    entity_type_filter=['class', 'function'],\n    dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n    2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n    direction='upstream',\n    traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n    Notes:\n    * Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n    * Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n    * Graph Context: The function operates on a pre-built code graph containing entities (e.g., files, classes and functions) and dependencies representing their interactions and relationships.\n    Parameters:\n    ----------\n    start_entities : list[str]\n    List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\n    - Entities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\"\n    (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\n    - For files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n    direction : str, optional\n    Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are:\n    - 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n    - 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others\n    (how others depend on them).\n    - 'both': Traversal in both directions.\n    Default is 'downstream'.\n    traversal_depth : int, optional\n    Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).\n    Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n    Default is 2.\n    entity_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all entity types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    dependency_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all dependency types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    Returns:\n    -------\n    result : object\n    An object representing the traversal results, which includes discovered entities and their dependencies.\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n\n- Comprehensive Dependency Analysis: Prioritize analyzing the code dependencies of functions, methods, or classes mentioned in the problem description or those identified in relevant locations. Use `search_invoke_and_reference` as the primary tool to ensure all interactions within the codebase are captured.\n\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many commands OR too much code in one \"execute\" block.\nInclude ONLY ONE <execute_ipython> per response, unless the assistant is finished with the task or needs more input or action from the user in order to proceed.\nIf the assistant is finished with the task you MUST include <finish></finish> in your response.\nIMPORTANT: Execute code using <execute_ipython> whenever possible.\nThe assistant should utilize full file paths to prevent path-related errors.\nThe assistant must avoid apologies and thanks in its responses.\nYou have access to the following functions:\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #1: finish ----\nDescription: Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task.\nNo parameters are required for this function.\n---- END FUNCTION #1 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #2: search_code_snippets ----\nDescription: Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n\nParameters:\n  (1) search_terms (array, optional): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (2) line_nums (array, optional): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (3) file_path_or_pattern (string, optional): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n---- END FUNCTION #2 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #3: get_entity_contents ----\nDescription: \nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n\nParameters:\n  (1) entity_names (array, required): A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n---- END FUNCTION #3 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #4: explore_tree_structure ----\nDescription: \nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n\nParameters:\n  (1) start_entities (array, required): List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n  (2) direction (string, optional): Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.\nAllowed values: [`upstream`, `downstream`, `both`]\n  (3) traversal_depth (integer, optional): Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n  (4) entity_type_filter (['array', 'null'], optional): List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.\n  (5) dependency_type_filter (['array', 'null'], optional): List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.\n---- END FUNCTION #4 ----\n\n\nIf you choose to call a function ONLY reply in the following format with NO suffix:\n\n<function=example_function_name>\n<parameter=example_parameter_1>value_1</parameter>\n<parameter=example_parameter_2>\nThis is the value for the second parameter\nthat can span\nmultiple lines\n</parameter>\n</function>\n\n<IMPORTANT>\nReminder:\n- Function calls MUST follow the specified format, start with <function= and end with </function>\n- Required parameters MUST be specified\n- Only call one function at a time\n- You may provide optional reasoning for your function call in natural language BEFORE the function call, but NOT after.\n- If there is no function call available, answer the question like normal with your current knowledge and do not tell the user about function calls\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "\nGiven the following GitHub issue description, your objective is to localize the specific files, classes or functions, and lines of code that are relevant to the solution for the issue. More specifically, these issues are all \"feature request\" issues, which means that you need to find the locations the feature described in the issue.\n\nFollow these steps to localize the issue:\n## Step 1: Categorize and Extract Key Problem Information\n - Classify the issue description into the following categories:\n    Requested feature description, behavior with out this feature, expected behavior with the feature, and additional context.\n - Identify modules in the 'scikit-learn' package mentioned in each category.\n - Use extracted keywords and line numbers to search for relevant code references for additional context.\n\n## Step 2: Locate Referenced Modules\n- Accurately determine specific modules\n    - Explore the repo to familiarize yourself with its structure.\n    - Analyze the described workflow to identify specific modules or components being referenced.\n- Pay special attention to distinguishing between modules with similar names using context and described workflow.\n- Output Format for collected relevant modules:\n    - Use the format: 'file_path:QualifiedName'\n    - E.g., for a function `calculate_sum` in the `MathUtils` class located in `src/helpers/math_helpers.py`, represent it as: 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'.\n\n## Step 3: Analyze the feature\n- Understand the Purpose of the Feature in the Context of the Codebase\n    - Understand what the feature is supposed to do.\n    - Understand why it is needed.\n    - Understand how it fits into the overall functionality of the codebase.\n- Analyze the Feature's Relationship with the Codebase\n    - Identify the key affected components of the feature.\n    - Understand how these components interact with each other and with existing code.\n    - Consider the inputs, outputs, and side effects of the feature.\n\n## Step 4: Locate Areas for Feature Implementation\n- Locate specific files, functions, or lines of code implementing the key part of the requested feature or containing critical information for resolving the issue.\n- Consider upstream and downstream dependencies that may affect or be affected by the issue.\n- Think Thoroughly: List multiple potential solutions and consider edge cases that could impact the resolution.\n\n## Output Format for Final Results:\nYour final output should list the locations requiring modification, wrapped with triple backticks ```\nEach location should include the file path, class name (if applicable), function name, or line numbers, ordered by importance.\nYour answer would better include about 5 files.\n\nIMPORTANT FORMAT RULES:\n- Output ONLY the location block, followed by `<finish></finish>`.\n- Do NOT include explanations, bullets, headings, numbering, summaries, or notes before or after the location block.\n- Each file path MUST appear on its own line and end with `.py`.\n- Do NOT write `file.py:QualifiedName` on a single line in the final answer.\n- After a file path line, use separate `line:`, `class:`, and `function:` lines as needed.\n\n### Examples:\n```\nfull_path1/file1.py\nline: 10\nclass: MyClass1\nfunction: my_function1\n\nfull_path2/file2.py\nline: 76\nfunction: MyClass2.my_function2\n\nfull_path3/file3.py\nline: 24\nline: 156\nfunction: my_function3\n```\n\nReturn just the location(s)\n\nNote: Your thinking should be thorough and so it's fine if it's very long.\n\n--- BEGIN PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\nTitle: Validate MD5 checksum of downloaded ARFF in fetch_openml\n\n`fetch_openml` downloads the `data_set_description` from openml.org which includes an `md5_checksum` field. This should correspond to the MD5 checksum of the downloaded ARFF data. We should (optionally?) use `hashlib` to verify that the MD5 matches.\n--- END PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\n\nIMPORTANT: You should ONLY interact with the environment provided to you AND NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\nDon't include any lambda functions!\nYou should NOT modify any files!\n"}, {"content": "Let's categorize and extract key problem information from the issue description:\n\nRequested feature description: Validate MD5 checksum of downloaded ARFF in `fetch_openml`.\nBehavior without this feature: The `md5_checksum` field from `data_set_description` is not verified against the downloaded ARFF data.\nExpected behavior with the feature: The `md5_checksum` field should be optionally verified against the MD5 checksum of the downloaded ARFF data.\nAdditional context: The verification can be done using the `hashlib` library.\n\nNext, let's identify modules in the `scikit-learn` package mentioned in the issue description:\n- `fetch_openml` (likely a function in the `sklearn.datasets` module)\n- `hashlib` (external library, no need to locate)\n\nWe will now locate the `fetch_openml` function to understand where modifications should be made.\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>\n['sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:fetch_openml']\n</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:fetch_openml`...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:fetch_openml`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:fetch_openml`.\n```\n664 | def fetch_openml(\n665 |     name: Optional[str] = None,\n666 |     *,\n667 |     version: Union[str, int] = 'active',\n668 |     data_id: Optional[int] = None,\n669 |     data_home: Optional[str] = None,\n670 |     target_column: Optional[Union[str, List]] = 'default-target',\n671 |     cache: bool = True,\n672 |     return_X_y: bool = False,\n673 |     as_frame: bool = False\n674 | ):\n675 |     \"\"\"Fetch dataset from openml by name or dataset id.\n676 | \n677 |     Datasets are uniquely identified by either an integer ID or by a\n678 |     combination of name and version (i.e. there might be multiple\n679 |     versions of the 'iris' dataset). Please give either name or data_id\n680 |     (not both). In case a name is given, a version can also be\n681 |     provided.\n682 | \n683 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <openml>`.\n684 | \n685 |     .. versionadded:: 0.20\n686 | \n687 |     .. note:: EXPERIMENTAL\n688 | \n689 |         The API is experimental (particularly the return value structure),\n690 |         and might have small backward-incompatible changes in future releases.\n691 | \n692 |     Parameters\n693 |     ----------\n694 |     name : str, default=None\n695 |         String identifier of the dataset. Note that OpenML can have multiple\n696 |         datasets with the same name.\n697 | \n698 |     version : int or 'active', default='active'\n699 |         Version of the dataset. Can only be provided if also ``name`` is given.\n700 |         If 'active' the oldest version that's still active is used. Since\n701 |         there may be more than one active version of a dataset, and those\n702 |         versions may fundamentally be different from one another, setting an\n703 |         exact version is highly recommended.\n704 | \n705 |     data_id : int, default=None\n706 |         OpenML ID of the dataset. The most specific way of retrieving a\n707 |         dataset. If data_id is not given, name (and potential version) are\n708 |         used to obtain a dataset.\n709 | \n710 |     data_home : str, default=None\n711 |         Specify another download and cache folder for the data sets. By default\n712 |         all scikit-learn data is stored in '~/scikit_learn_data' subfolders.\n713 | \n714 |     target_column : str, list or None, default='default-target'\n715 |         Specify the column name in the data to use as target. If\n716 |         'default-target', the standard target column a stored on the server\n717 |         is used. If ``None``, all columns are returned as data and the\n718 |         target is ``None``. If list (of strings), all columns with these names\n719 |         are returned as multi-target (Note: not all scikit-learn classifiers\n720 |         can handle all types of multi-output combinations)\n721 | \n722 |     cache : bool, default=True\n723 |         Whether to cache downloaded datasets using joblib.\n724 | \n725 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False\n726 |         If True, returns ``(data, target)`` instead of a Bunch object. See\n727 |         below for more information about the `data` and `target` objects.\n728 | \n729 |     as_frame : bool or 'auto', default=False\n730 |         If True, the data is a pandas DataFrame including columns with\n731 |         appropriate dtypes (numeric, string or categorical). The target is\n732 |         a pandas DataFrame or Series depending on the number of target_columns.\n733 |         The Bunch will contain a ``frame`` attribute with the target and the\n734 |         data. If ``return_X_y`` is True, then ``(data, target)`` will be pandas\n735 |         DataFrames or Series as describe above.\n736 |         If as_frame is 'auto', the data and target will be converted to\n737 |         DataFrame or Series as if as_frame is set to True, unless the dataset\n738 |         is stored in sparse format.\n739 | \n740 |     Returns\n741 |     -------\n742 | \n743 |     data : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n744 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n745 | \n746 |         data : np.array, scipy.sparse.csr_matrix of floats, or pandas DataFrame\n747 |             The feature matrix. Categorical features are encoded as ordinals.\n748 |         target : np.array, pandas Series or DataFrame\n749 |             The regression target or classification labels, if applicable.\n750 |             Dtype is float if numeric, and object if categorical. If\n751 |             ``as_frame`` is True, ``target`` is a pandas object.\n752 |         DESCR : str\n753 |             The full description of the dataset\n754 |         feature_names : list\n755 |             The names of the dataset columns\n756 |         target_names: list\n757 |             The names of the target columns\n758 | \n759 |         .. versionadded:: 0.22\n760 | \n761 |         categories : dict or None\n762 |             Maps each categorical feature name to a list of values, such\n763 |             that the value encoded as i is ith in the list. If ``as_frame``\n764 |             is True, this is None.\n765 |         details : dict\n766 |             More metadata from OpenML\n767 |         frame : pandas DataFrame\n768 |             Only present when `as_frame=True`. DataFrame with ``data`` and\n769 |             ``target``.\n770 | \n771 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n772 | \n773 |         .. note:: EXPERIMENTAL\n774 | \n775 |             This interface is **experimental** and subsequent releases may\n776 |             change attributes without notice (although there should only be\n777 |             minor changes to ``data`` and ``target``).\n778 | \n779 |         Missing values in the 'data' are represented as NaN's. Missing values\n780 |         in 'target' are represented as NaN's (numerical target) or None\n781 |         (categorical target)\n782 |     \"\"\"\n783 |     data_home = get_data_home(data_home=data_home)\n784 |     data_home = join(data_home, 'openml')\n785 |     if cache is False:\n786 |         # no caching will be applied\n787 |         data_home = None\n788 | \n789 |     # check valid function arguments. data_id XOR (name, version) should be\n790 |     # provided\n791 |     if name is not None:\n792 |         # OpenML is case-insensitive, but the caching mechanism is not\n793 |         # convert all data names (str) to lower case\n794 |         name = name.lower()\n795 |         if data_id is not None:\n796 |             raise ValueError(\n797 |                 \"Dataset data_id={} and name={} passed, but you can only \"\n798 |                 \"specify a numeric data_id or a name, not \"\n799 |                 \"both.\".format(data_id, name))\n800 |         data_info = _get_data_info_by_name(name, version, data_home)\n801 |         data_id = data_info['did']\n802 |     elif data_id is not None:\n803 |         # from the previous if statement, it is given that name is None\n804 |         if version != \"active\":\n805 |             raise ValueError(\n806 |                 \"Dataset data_id={} and version={} passed, but you can only \"\n807 |                 \"specify a numeric data_id or a version, not \"\n808 |                 \"both.\".format(data_id, name))\n809 |     else:\n810 |         raise ValueError(\n811 |             \"Neither name nor data_id are provided. Please provide name or \"\n812 |             \"data_id.\")\n813 | \n814 |     data_description = _get_data_description_by_id(data_id, data_home)\n815 |     if data_description['status'] != \"active\":\n816 |         warn(\"Version {} of dataset {} is inactive, meaning that issues have \"\n817 |              \"been found in the dataset. Try using a newer version from \"\n818 |              \"this URL: {}\".format(\n819 |                 data_description['version'],\n820 |                 data_description['name'],\n821 |                 data_description['url']))\n822 |     if 'error' in data_description:\n823 |         warn(\"OpenML registered a problem with the dataset. It might be \"\n824 |              \"unusable. Error: {}\".format(data_description['error']))\n825 |     if 'warning' in data_description:\n826 |         warn(\"OpenML raised a warning on the dataset. It might be \"\n827 |              \"unusable. Warning: {}\".format(data_description['warning']))\n828 | \n829 |     return_sparse = False\n830 |     if data_description['format'].lower() == 'sparse_arff':\n831 |         return_sparse = True\n832 | \n833 |     if as_frame == 'auto':\n834 |         as_frame = not return_sparse\n835 | \n836 |     if as_frame and return_sparse:\n837 |         raise ValueError('Cannot return dataframe with sparse data')\n838 | \n839 |     # download data features, meta-info about column types\n840 |     features_list = _get_data_features(data_id, data_home)\n841 | \n842 |     if not as_frame:\n843 |         for feature in features_list:\n844 |             if 'true' in (feature['is_ignore'], feature['is_row_identifier']):\n845 |                 continue\n846 |             if feature['data_type'] == 'string':\n847 |                 raise ValueError('STRING attributes are not supported for '\n848 |                                  'array representation. Try as_frame=True')\n849 | \n850 |     if target_column == \"default-target\":\n851 |         # determines the default target based on the data feature results\n852 |         # (which is currently more reliable than the data description;\n853 |         # see issue: https://github.com/openml/OpenML/issues/768)\n854 |         target_columns = [feature['name'] for feature in features_list\n855 |                           if feature['is_target'] == 'true']\n856 |     elif isinstance(target_column, str):\n857 |         # for code-simplicity, make target_column by default a list\n858 |         target_columns = [target_column]\n859 |     elif target_column is None:\n860 |         target_columns = []\n861 |     elif isinstance(target_column, list):\n862 |         target_columns = target_column\n863 |     else:\n864 |         raise TypeError(\"Did not recognize type of target_column\"\n865 |                         \"Should be str, list or None. Got: \"\n866 |                         \"{}\".format(type(target_column)))\n867 |     data_columns = _valid_data_column_names(features_list,\n868 |                                             target_columns)\n869 | \n870 |     shape: Optional[Tuple[int, int]]\n871 |     # determine arff encoding to return\n872 |     if not return_sparse:\n873 |         # The shape must include the ignored features to keep the right indexes\n874 |         # during the arff data conversion.\n875 |         data_qualities = _get_data_qualities(data_id, data_home)\n876 |         shape = _get_num_samples(data_qualities), len(features_list)\n877 |     else:\n878 |         shape = None\n879 | \n880 |     # obtain the data\n881 |     url = _DATA_FILE.format(data_description['file_id'])\n882 |     bunch = _download_data_to_bunch(url, return_sparse, data_home,\n883 |                                     as_frame=as_frame,\n884 |                                     features_list=features_list, shape=shape,\n885 |                                     target_columns=target_columns,\n886 |                                     data_columns=data_columns)\n887 | \n888 |     if return_X_y:\n889 |         return bunch.data, bunch.target\n890 | \n891 |     description = \"{}\\n\\nDownloaded from openml.org.\".format(\n892 |         data_description.pop('description'))\n893 | \n894 |     bunch.update(\n895 |         DESCR=description, details=data_description,\n896 |         url=\"https://www.openml.org/d/{}\".format(data_id))\n897 | \n898 |     return bunch\n```\n"}, {"content": "The `fetch_openml` function is located in `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py`. We need to focus on the part where the ARFF data is downloaded and processed. Specifically, we need to find where the ARFF file is downloaded and how the `data_description` is handled.\n\nLet's look at the `_download_data_to_bunch` function, which seems to handle the downloading of the data file, and the `_get_data_description_by_id` function, which retrieves the `data_description` including the `md5_checksum`.\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>\n['sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_download_data_to_bunch', 'sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_description_by_id']\n</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_download_data_to_bunch`...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_download_data_to_bunch`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_download_data_to_bunch`.\n```\n509 | def _download_data_to_bunch(\n510 |     url: str,\n511 |     sparse: bool,\n512 |     data_home: Optional[str],\n513 |     *,\n514 |     as_frame: bool,\n515 |     features_list: List,\n516 |     data_columns: List[int],\n517 |     target_columns: List,\n518 |     shape: Optional[Tuple[int, int]]\n519 | ):\n520 |     \"\"\"Download OpenML ARFF and convert to Bunch of data\n521 |     \"\"\"\n522 |     # NB: this function is long in order to handle retry for any failure\n523 |     #     during the streaming parse of the ARFF.\n524 | \n525 |     # Prepare which columns and data types should be returned for the X and y\n526 |     features_dict = {feature['name']: feature for feature in features_list}\n527 | \n528 |     # XXX: col_slice_y should be all nominal or all numeric\n529 |     _verify_target_data_type(features_dict, target_columns)\n530 | \n531 |     col_slice_y = [int(features_dict[col_name]['index'])\n532 |                    for col_name in target_columns]\n533 | \n534 |     col_slice_x = [int(features_dict[col_name]['index'])\n535 |                    for col_name in data_columns]\n536 |     for col_idx in col_slice_y:\n537 |         feat = features_list[col_idx]\n538 |         nr_missing = int(feat['number_of_missing_values'])\n539 |         if nr_missing > 0:\n540 |             raise ValueError('Target column {} has {} missing values. '\n541 |                              'Missing values are not supported for target '\n542 |                              'columns. '.format(feat['name'], nr_missing))\n543 | \n544 |     # Access an ARFF file on the OpenML server. Documentation:\n545 |     # https://www.openml.org/api_data_docs#!/data/get_download_id\n546 | \n547 |     if sparse is True:\n548 |         return_type = _arff.COO\n549 |     else:\n550 |         return_type = _arff.DENSE_GEN\n551 | \n552 |     frame = nominal_attributes = None\n553 | \n554 |     parse_arff: Callable\n555 |     postprocess: Callable\n556 |     if as_frame:\n557 |         columns = data_columns + target_columns\n558 |         parse_arff = partial(_convert_arff_data_dataframe, columns=columns,\n559 |                              features_dict=features_dict)\n560 | \n561 |         def postprocess(frame):\n562 |             X = frame[data_columns]\n563 |             if len(target_columns) >= 2:\n564 |                 y = frame[target_columns]\n565 |             elif len(target_columns) == 1:\n566 |                 y = frame[target_columns[0]]\n567 |             else:\n568 |                 y = None\n569 |             return X, y, frame, nominal_attributes\n570 |     else:\n571 |         def parse_arff(arff):\n572 |             X, y = _convert_arff_data(arff, col_slice_x, col_slice_y, shape)\n573 |             # nominal attributes is a dict mapping from the attribute name to\n574 |             # the possible values. Includes also the target column (which will\n575 |             # be popped off below, before it will be packed in the Bunch\n576 |             # object)\n577 |             nominal_attributes = {k: v for k, v in arff['attributes']\n578 |                                   if isinstance(v, list) and\n579 |                                   k in data_columns + target_columns}\n580 |             return X, y, nominal_attributes\n581 | \n582 |         def postprocess(X, y, nominal_attributes):\n583 |             is_classification = {col_name in nominal_attributes\n584 |                                  for col_name in target_columns}\n585 |             if not is_classification:\n586 |                 # No target\n587 |                 pass\n588 |             elif all(is_classification):\n589 |                 y = np.hstack([\n590 |                     np.take(\n591 |                         np.asarray(nominal_attributes.pop(col_name),\n592 |                                    dtype='O'),\n593 |                         y[:, i:i + 1].astype(int, copy=False))\n594 |                     for i, col_name in enumerate(target_columns)\n595 |                 ])\n596 |             elif any(is_classification):\n597 |                 raise ValueError('Mix of nominal and non-nominal targets is '\n598 |                                  'not currently supported')\n599 | \n600 |             # reshape y back to 1-D array, if there is only 1 target column;\n601 |             # back to None if there are not target columns\n602 |             if y.shape[1] == 1:\n603 |                 y = y.reshape((-1,))\n604 |             elif y.shape[1] == 0:\n605 |                 y = None\n606 |             return X, y, frame, nominal_attributes\n607 | \n608 |     out = _retry_with_clean_cache(url, data_home)(\n609 |         _load_arff_response)(url, data_home,\n610 |                              return_type=return_type,\n611 |                              encode_nominal=not as_frame,\n612 |                              parse_arff=parse_arff)\n613 |     X, y, frame, nominal_attributes = postprocess(*out)\n614 | \n615 |     return Bunch(data=X, target=y, frame=frame,\n616 |                  categories=nominal_attributes,\n617 |                  feature_names=data_columns,\n618 |                  target_names=target_columns)\n```\n\n\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_description_by_id`...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_description_by_id`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_description_by_id`.\n```\n430 | def _get_data_description_by_id(\n431 |     data_id: int, data_home: Optional[str]\n432 | ) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n433 |     # OpenML API function: https://www.openml.org/api_docs#!/data/get_data_id\n434 |     url = _DATA_INFO.format(data_id)\n435 |     error_message = \"Dataset with data_id {} not found.\".format(data_id)\n436 |     json_data = _get_json_content_from_openml_api(\n437 |         url, error_message, data_home=data_home\n438 |     )\n439 |     return json_data['data_set_description']\n```\n"}, {"content": "From the `_download_data_to_bunch` function, we see that the ARFF data is downloaded and processed. The `_get_data_description_by_id` function retrieves the dataset description, which includes the `md5_checksum`.\n\nTo implement the feature of validating the MD5 checksum, we need to add logic in `_download_data_to_bunch` to calculate the checksum of the downloaded file and compare it with the `md5_checksum` from `_get_data_description_by_id`.\n\nLet's also check where the ARFF file is saved locally, as we need to read the file to calculate the checksum.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>\n['save_arff', '_load_arff_response']\n</parameter>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>\n'sklearn/datasets/_openml.py'\n</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nsklearn/utils/validation.py:_check_fit_params\nsklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:fetch_rcv1\nsklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:fetch_rcv1\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py:fetch_openml\nsklearn/datasets/_samples_generator.py:make_classification\nsklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:fetch_rcv1\nsklearn/datasets/_samples_generator.py:make_classification\nNo files found for file pattern ''sklearn/datasets/_openml.py''. Will search all files.\n...\n##Searching for term \"save_arff\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound code snippet in file `sklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"RCV1 dataset.\n  2 | \n  3 | The dataset page is available at\n  4 | \n  5 |     http://jmlr.csail.mit.edu/papers/volume5/lewis04a/\n  6 | \"\"\"\n  7 | \n  8 | # Author: Tom Dupre la Tour\n  9 | # License: BSD 3 clause\n 10 | \n 11 | import logging\n 12 | \n 13 | from os import remove, makedirs\n 14 | from os.path import dirname, exists, join\n 15 | from gzip import GzipFile\n 16 | \n 17 | import numpy as np\n 18 | import scipy.sparse as sp\n 19 | import joblib\n 20 | \n 21 | from . import get_data_home\n 22 | from ._base import _pkl_filepath\n 23 | from ._base import _fetch_remote\n 24 | from ._base import RemoteFileMetadata\n 25 | from ._svmlight_format_io import load_svmlight_files\n 26 | from ..utils import shuffle as shuffle_\n 27 | from ..utils import Bunch\n 28 | from ..utils.validation import _deprecate_positional_args\n 29 | \n 30 | \n 31 | # The original vectorized data can be found at:\n 32 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a13-vector-files/lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt0.dat.gz\n 33 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a13-vector-files/lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt1.dat.gz\n 34 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a13-vector-files/lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt2.dat.gz\n 35 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a13-vector-files/lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt3.dat.gz\n 36 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a13-vector-files/lyrl2004_vectors_train.dat.gz\n 37 | # while the original stemmed token files can be found\n 38 | # in the README, section B.12.i.:\n 39 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/lyrl2004_rcv1v2_README.htm\n```\n\nFound .\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 40 | XY_METADATA = (\n 41 |     RemoteFileMetadata(\n 42 |         url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976069',\n 43 |         checksum=('ed40f7e418d10484091b059703eeb95a'\n 44 |                   'e3199fe042891dcec4be6696b9968374'),\n 45 |         filename='lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt0.dat.gz'),\n 46 |     RemoteFileMetadata(\n 47 |         url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976066',\n 48 |         checksum=('87700668ae45d45d5ca1ef6ae9bd81ab'\n 49 |                   '0f5ec88cc95dcef9ae7838f727a13aa6'),\n 50 |         filename='lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt1.dat.gz'),\n 51 |     RemoteFileMetadata(\n 52 |         url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976063',\n 53 |         checksum=('48143ac703cbe33299f7ae9f4995db4'\n 54 |                   '9a258690f60e5debbff8995c34841c7f5'),\n 55 |         filename='lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt2.dat.gz'),\n 56 |     RemoteFileMetadata(\n 57 |         url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976060',\n 58 |         checksum=('dfcb0d658311481523c6e6ca0c3f5a3'\n 59 |                   'e1d3d12cde5d7a8ce629a9006ec7dbb39'),\n 60 |         filename='lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt3.dat.gz'),\n 61 |     RemoteFileMetadata(\n 62 |         url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976057',\n 63 |         checksum=('5468f656d0ba7a83afc7ad44841cf9a5'\n 64 |                   '3048a5c083eedc005dcdb5cc768924ae'),\n 65 |         filename='lyrl2004_vectors_train.dat.gz')\n 66 | )\n 67 | \n 68 | # The original data can be found at:\n 69 | # http://jmlr.csail.mit.edu/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a08-topic-qrels/rcv1-v2.topics.qrels.gz\n 70 | TOPICS_METADATA = RemoteFileMetadata(\n 71 |     url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976048',\n 72 |     checksum=('2a98e5e5d8b770bded93afc8930d882'\n 73 |               '99474317fe14181aee1466cc754d0d1c1'),\n 74 |     filename='rcv1v2.topics.qrels.gz')\n 75 | \n 76 | logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:fetch_rcv1`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 80 | def fetch_rcv1(*, data_home=None, subset='all', download_if_missing=True,\n 81 |                random_state=None, shuffle=False, return_X_y=False):\n 82 |     \"\"\"Load the RCV1 multilabel dataset (classification).\n 83 | \n 84 |     Download it if necessary.\n 85 | \n 86 |     Version: RCV1-v2, vectors, full sets, topics multilabels.\n 87 | \n 88 |     =================   =====================\n 89 |     Classes                               103\n 90 |     Samples total                      804414\n 91 |     Dimensionality                      47236\n 92 |     Features            real, between 0 and 1\n 93 |     =================   =====================\n 94 | \n 95 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <rcv1_dataset>`.\n 96 | \n 97 |     .. versionadded:: 0.17\n 98 | \n 99 |     Parameters\n100 |     ----------\n101 |     data_home : str, default=None\n102 |         Specify another download and cache folder for the datasets. By default\n103 |         all scikit-learn data is stored in '~/scikit_learn_data' subfolders.\n104 | \n105 |     subset : {'train', 'test', 'all'}, default='all'\n106 |         Select the dataset to load: 'train' for the training set\n107 |         (23149 samples), 'test' for the test set (781265 samples),\n108 |         'all' for both, with the training samples first if shuffle is False.\n109 |         This follows the official LYRL2004 chronological split.\n110 | \n111 |     download_if_missing : bool, default=True\n112 |         If False, raise a IOError if the data is not locally available\n113 |         instead of trying to download the data from the source site.\n114 | \n115 |     random_state : int or RandomState instance, default=None\n116 |         Determines random number generation for dataset shuffling. Pass an int\n117 |         for reproducible output across multiple function calls.\n118 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n119 | \n120 |     shuffle : bool, default=False\n121 |         Whether to shuffle dataset.\n122 | \n123 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False\n124 |         If True, returns ``(dataset.data, dataset.target)`` instead of a Bunch\n125 |         object. See below for more information about the `dataset.data` and\n126 |         `dataset.target` object.\n127 | \n128 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n129 | \n130 |     Returns\n131 |     -------\n132 |     dataset : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n133 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n134 | \n135 |         data : scipy csr array, dtype np.float64, shape (804414, 47236)\n136 |             The array has 0.16% of non zero values.\n137 |         target : scipy csr array, dtype np.uint8, shape (804414, 103)\n138 |             Each sample has a value of 1 in its categories, and 0 in others.\n139 |             The array has 3.15% of non zero values.\n140 |         sample_id : numpy array, dtype np.uint32, shape (804414,)\n141 |             Identification number of each sample, as ordered in dataset.data.\n142 |         target_names : numpy array, dtype object, length (103)\n143 |             Names of each target (RCV1 topics), as ordered in dataset.target.\n144 |         DESCR : string\n145 |             Description of the RCV1 dataset.\n146 | \n147 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n148 | \n149 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n150 |     \"\"\"\n151 |     N_SAMPLES = 804414\n152 |     N_FEATURES = 47236\n153 |     N_CATEGORIES = 103\n154 |     N_TRAIN = 23149\n155 | \n156 |     data_home = get_data_home(data_home=data_home)\n157 |     rcv1_dir = join(data_home, \"RCV1\")\n158 |     if download_if_missing:\n159 |         if not exists(rcv1_dir):\n160 |             makedirs(rcv1_dir)\n161 | \n162 |     samples_path = _pkl_filepath(rcv1_dir, \"samples.pkl\")\n163 |     sample_id_path = _pkl_filepath(rcv1_dir, \"sample_id.pkl\")\n164 |     sample_topics_path = _pkl_filepath(rcv1_dir, \"sample_topics.pkl\")\n165 |     topics_path = _pkl_filepath(rcv1_dir, \"topics_names.pkl\")\n166 | \n167 |     # load data (X) and sample_id\n168 |     if download_if_missing and (not exists(samples_path) or\n169 |                                 not exists(sample_id_path)):\n170 |         files = []\n171 |         for each in XY_METADATA:\n172 |             logger.info(\"Downloading %s\" % each.url)\n173 |             file_path = _fetch_remote(each, dirname=rcv1_dir)\n174 |             files.append(GzipFile(filename=file_path))\n175 | \n176 |         Xy = load_svmlight_files(files, n_features=N_FEATURES)\n177 | \n178 |         # Training data is before testing data\n179 |         X = sp.vstack([Xy[8], Xy[0], Xy[2], Xy[4], Xy[6]]).tocsr()\n180 |         sample_id = np.hstack((Xy[9], Xy[1], Xy[3], Xy[5], Xy[7]))\n181 |         sample_id = sample_id.astype(np.uint32, copy=False)\n182 | \n183 |         joblib.dump(X, samples_path, compress=9)\n184 |         joblib.dump(sample_id, sample_id_path, compress=9)\n185 | \n186 |         # delete archives\n187 |         for f in files:\n188 |             f.close()\n189 |             remove(f.name)\n190 |     else:\n191 |         X = joblib.load(samples_path)\n192 |         sample_id = joblib.load(sample_id_path)\n193 | \n194 |     # load target (y), categories, and sample_id_bis\n195 |     if download_if_missing and (not exists(sample_topics_path) or\n196 |                                 not exists(topics_path)):\n197 |         logger.info(\"Downloading %s\" % TOPICS_METADATA.url)\n198 |         topics_archive_path = _fetch_remote(TOPICS_METADATA,\n199 |                                             dirname=rcv1_dir)\n200 | \n201 |         # parse the target file\n202 |         n_cat = -1\n203 |         n_doc = -1\n204 |         doc_previous = -1\n205 |         y = np.zeros((N_SAMPLES, N_CATEGORIES), dtype=np.uint8)\n206 |         sample_id_bis = np.zeros(N_SAMPLES, dtype=np.int32)\n207 |         category_names = {}\n208 |         with GzipFile(filename=topics_archive_path, mode='rb') as f:\n209 |             for line in f:\n210 |                 line_components = line.decode(\"ascii\").split(\" \")\n211 |                 if len(line_components) == 3:\n212 |                     cat, doc, _ = line_components\n213 |                     if cat not in category_names:\n214 |                         n_cat += 1\n215 |                         category_names[cat] = n_cat\n216 | \n217 |                     doc = int(doc)\n218 |                     if doc != doc_previous:\n219 |                         doc_previous = doc\n220 |                         n_doc += 1\n221 |                         sample_id_bis[n_doc] = doc\n222 |                     y[n_doc, category_names[cat]] = 1\n223 | \n224 |         # delete archive\n225 |         remove(topics_archive_path)\n226 | \n227 |         # Samples in X are ordered with sample_id,\n228 |         # whereas in y, they are ordered with sample_id_bis.\n229 |         permutation = _find_permutation(sample_id_bis, sample_id)\n230 |         y = y[permutation, :]\n231 | \n232 |         # save category names in a list, with same order than y\n233 |         categories = np.empty(N_CATEGORIES, dtype=object)\n234 |         for k in category_names.keys():\n235 |             categories[category_names[k]] = k\n236 | \n237 |         # reorder categories in lexicographic order\n238 |         order = np.argsort(categories)\n239 |         categories = categories[order]\n240 |         y = sp.csr_matrix(y[:, order])\n241 | \n242 |         joblib.dump(y, sample_topics_path, compress=9)\n243 |         joblib.dump(categories, topics_path, compress=9)\n244 |     else:\n245 |         y = joblib.load(sample_topics_path)\n246 |         categories = joblib.load(topics_path)\n247 | \n248 |     if subset == 'all':\n249 |         pass\n250 |     elif subset == 'train':\n251 |         X = X[:N_TRAIN, :]\n252 |         y = y[:N_TRAIN, :]\n253 |         sample_id = sample_id[:N_TRAIN]\n254 |     elif subset == 'test':\n255 |         X = X[N_TRAIN:, :]\n256 |         y = y[N_TRAIN:, :]\n257 |         sample_id = sample_id[N_TRAIN:]\n258 |     else:\n259 |         raise ValueError(\"Unknown subset parameter. Got '%s' instead of one\"\n260 |                          \" of ('all', 'train', test')\" % subset)\n261 | \n262 |     if shuffle:\n263 |         X, y, sample_id = shuffle_(X, y, sample_id, random_state=random_state)\n264 | \n265 |     module_path = dirname(__file__)\n266 |     with open(join(module_path, 'descr', 'rcv1.rst')) as rst_file:\n267 |         fdescr = rst_file.read()\n268 | \n269 |     if return_X_y:\n270 |         return X, y\n271 | \n272 |     return Bunch(data=X, target=y, sample_id=sample_id,\n273 |                  target_names=categories, DESCR=fdescr)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_convert_arff_data`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n233 | def _convert_arff_data(\n234 |     arff: ArffContainerType,\n235 |     col_slice_x: List[int],\n236 |     col_slice_y: List[int],\n237 |     shape: Optional[Tuple] = None\n238 | ) -> Tuple:\n239 |     \"\"\"\n240 |     converts the arff object into the appropriate matrix type (np.array or\n241 |     scipy.sparse.csr_matrix) based on the 'data part' (i.e., in the\n242 |     liac-arff dict, the object from the 'data' key)\n243 | \n244 |     Parameters\n245 |     ----------\n246 |     arff : dict\n247 |         As obtained from liac-arff object.\n248 | \n249 |     col_slice_x : list\n250 |         The column indices that are sliced from the original array to return\n251 |         as X data\n252 | \n253 |     col_slice_y : list\n254 |         The column indices that are sliced from the original array to return\n255 |         as y data\n256 | \n257 |     Returns\n258 |     -------\n259 |     X : np.array or scipy.sparse.csr_matrix\n260 |     y : np.array\n261 |     \"\"\"\n262 |     arff_data = arff['data']\n263 |     if isinstance(arff_data, Generator):\n264 |         if shape is None:\n265 |             raise ValueError(\n266 |                 \"shape must be provided when arr['data'] is a Generator\"\n267 |             )\n268 |         if shape[0] == -1:\n269 |             count = -1\n270 |         else:\n271 |             count = shape[0] * shape[1]\n272 |         data = np.fromiter(itertools.chain.from_iterable(arff_data),\n273 |                            dtype='float64', count=count)\n274 |         data = data.reshape(*shape)\n275 |         X = data[:, col_slice_x]\n276 |         y = data[:, col_slice_y]\n277 |         return X, y\n278 |     elif isinstance(arff_data, tuple):\n279 |         arff_data_X = _split_sparse_columns(arff_data, col_slice_x)\n280 |         num_obs = max(arff_data[1]) + 1\n281 |         X_shape = (num_obs, len(col_slice_x))\n282 |         X = scipy.sparse.coo_matrix(\n283 |             (arff_data_X[0], (arff_data_X[1], arff_data_X[2])),\n284 |             shape=X_shape, dtype=np.float64)\n285 |         X = X.tocsr()\n286 |         y = _sparse_data_to_array(arff_data, col_slice_y)\n287 |         return X, y\n288 |     else:\n289 |         # This should never happen\n290 |         raise ValueError('Unexpected Data Type obtained from arff.')\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_convert_arff_data_dataframe`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n310 | def _convert_arff_data_dataframe(\n311 |     arff: ArffContainerType, columns: List, features_dict: Dict[str, Any]\n312 | ) -> Tuple:\n313 |     \"\"\"Convert the ARFF object into a pandas DataFrame.\n314 | \n315 |     Parameters\n316 |     ----------\n317 |     arff : dict\n318 |         As obtained from liac-arff object.\n319 | \n320 |     columns : list\n321 |         Columns from dataframe to return.\n322 | \n323 |     features_dict : dict\n324 |         Maps feature name to feature info from openml.\n325 | \n326 |     Returns\n327 |     -------\n328 |     result : tuple\n329 |         tuple with the resulting dataframe\n330 |     \"\"\"\n331 |     pd = check_pandas_support('fetch_openml with as_frame=True')\n332 | \n333 |     attributes = OrderedDict(arff['attributes'])\n334 |     arff_columns = list(attributes)\n335 | \n336 |     if not isinstance(arff['data'], Generator):\n337 |         raise ValueError(\n338 |             \"arff['data'] must be a generator when converting to pd.DataFrame.\"\n339 |         )\n340 | \n341 |     # calculate chunksize\n342 |     first_row = next(arff['data'])\n343 |     first_df = pd.DataFrame([first_row], columns=arff_columns)\n344 | \n345 |     row_bytes = first_df.memory_usage(deep=True).sum()\n346 |     chunksize = get_chunk_n_rows(row_bytes)\n347 | \n348 |     # read arff data with chunks\n349 |     columns_to_keep = [col for col in arff_columns if col in columns]\n350 |     dfs = []\n351 |     dfs.append(first_df[columns_to_keep])\n352 |     for data in _chunk_generator(arff['data'], chunksize):\n353 |         dfs.append(pd.DataFrame(data, columns=arff_columns)[columns_to_keep])\n354 |     df = pd.concat(dfs, ignore_index=True)\n355 | \n356 |     for column in columns_to_keep:\n357 |         dtype = _feature_to_dtype(features_dict[column])\n358 |         if dtype == 'category':\n359 |             dtype = pd.api.types.CategoricalDtype(attributes[column])\n360 |         df[column] = df[column].astype(dtype, copy=False)\n361 |     return (df, )\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/validation.py:_check_fit_params`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1344 | def _check_fit_params(X, fit_params, indices=None):\n1345 |     \"\"\"Check and validate the parameters passed during `fit`.\n1346 | \n1347 |     Parameters\n1348 |     ----------\n1349 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1350 |         Data array.\n1351 | \n1352 |     fit_params : dict\n1353 |         Dictionary containing the parameters passed at fit.\n1354 | \n1355 |     indices : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n1356 |         Indices to be selected if the parameter has the same size as `X`.\n1357 | \n1358 |     Returns\n1359 |     -------\n1360 |     fit_params_validated : dict\n1361 |         Validated parameters. We ensure that the values support indexing.\n1362 |     \"\"\"\n1363 |     from . import _safe_indexing\n1364 |     fit_params_validated = {}\n1365 |     for param_key, param_value in fit_params.items():\n1366 |         if (not _is_arraylike(param_value) or\n1367 |                 _num_samples(param_value) != _num_samples(X)):\n1368 |             # Non-indexable pass-through (for now for backward-compatibility).\n1369 |             # https://github.com/scikit-learn/scikit-learn/issues/15805\n1370 |             fit_params_validated[param_key] = param_value\n1371 |         else:\n1372 |             # Any other fit_params should support indexing\n1373 |             # (e.g. for cross-validation).\n1374 |             fit_params_validated[param_key] = _make_indexable(param_value)\n1375 |             fit_params_validated[param_key] = _safe_indexing(\n1376 |                 fit_params_validated[param_key], indices\n1377 |             )\n1378 | \n1379 |     return fit_params_validated\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"save_arff\", \"_load_arff_response\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_load_arff_response`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n491 | def _load_arff_response(\n492 |     url: str,\n493 |     data_home: Optional[str],\n494 |     return_type, encode_nominal: bool,\n495 |     parse_arff: Callable[[ArffContainerType], Tuple]\n496 | ) -> Tuple:\n497 |     \"\"\"Load arff data with url and parses arff response with parse_arff\"\"\"\n498 |     response = _open_openml_url(url, data_home)\n499 | \n500 |     with closing(response):\n501 |         # Note that if the data is dense, no reading is done until the data\n502 |         # generator is iterated.\n503 |         arff = _arff.load((line.decode('utf-8') for line in response),\n504 |                           return_type=return_type,\n505 |                           encode_nominal=encode_nominal)\n506 |         return parse_arff(arff)\n```\n"}, {"content": "Based on the search results, we need to focus on the `_load_arff_response` function in `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py` to add the MD5 checksum validation logic. This function handles the downloading and parsing of the ARFF data.\n\nWe also need to ensure that the `_get_data_description_by_id` function is correctly retrieving the `md5_checksum` from the `data_description` dictionary.\n\nAdditionally, we should check the `_download_data_to_bunch` function to understand where the ARFF file is being saved locally and how the data is being processed.\n\nLet's also explore the `_convert_arff_data` and `_convert_arff_data_dataframe` functions to see if any modifications are needed to handle the checksum validation.\n\nHere are the relevant locations for implementing the feature:\n\n```\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 491\nfunction: _load_arff_response\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 509\nfunction: _download_data_to_bunch\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 430\nfunction: _get_data_description_by_id\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 233\nfunction: _convert_arff_data\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 310\nfunction: _convert_arff_data_dataframe\n```\n\n<finish></finish>", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "explore_tree_structure", "description": "\nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"start_entities": {"description": "List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).", "type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "direction": {"description": "Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.", "type": "string", "enum": ["upstream", "downstream", "both"], "default": "downstream"}, "traversal_depth": {"description": "Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).", "type": "integer", "default": 2}, "entity_type_filter": {"description": "List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}, "dependency_type_filter": {"description": "List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}}, "required": ["start_entities"]}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 85525, "completion_tokens": 829}, "time": 271.9707672595978}]}}
{"instance_id": "pandas-dev__pandas-59900", "found_files": [["pandas/core/strings/accessor.py"]], "found_modules": [["pandas/core/strings/accessor.py:StringMethods", "pandas/io/pytables.py:Fixed", "pandas/core/frame.py:DataFrame", "pandas/io/pytables.py:GenericFixed"]], "found_entities": [["pandas/core/strings/accessor.py:StringMethods._validate", "pandas/core/strings/accessor.py:StringMethods.join", "pandas/io/pytables.py:Fixed.write", "pandas/core/strings/accessor.py:StringMethods.removeprefix", "pandas/core/frame.py:DataFrame.to_orc", "pandas/core/strings/accessor.py:StringMethods.removesuffix", "pandas/io/pytables.py:GenericFixed.write_multi_index", "pandas/core/strings/accessor.py:StringMethods.len"]], "raw_output_loc": ["```\npandas/core/strings/accessor.py\nline: 258\nfunction: _validate\n\npandas/core/strings/accessor.py\nline: 1158\nfunction: join\n\npandas/core/strings/accessor.py\nline: 2907\nfunction: removeprefix\n\npandas/core/strings/accessor.py\nline: 3005\nfunction: removesuffix\n\npandas/core/strings/accessor.py\nline: 3110\nfunction: len\n```"], "meta_data": {"repo": "pandas-dev/pandas", "base_commit": "8d2ca0bf84bcf44a800ac19bdb4ed7ec88c555e2", "problem_statement": "ENH: increase verbosity on error\n### Feature Type\n\n- [ ] Adding new functionality to pandas\n\n- [X] Changing existing functionality in pandas\n\n- [ ] Removing existing functionality in pandas\n\n\n### Problem Description\n\nOn a specific type error the error causing type is not logged in the error\n\n### Feature Description\n\nBy replacing:\r\n`raise AttributeError(\"Can only use .str accessor with string values!\")`\r\nwith\r\n`raise AttributeError(f\"Can only use .str accessor with string values! Inferred dType: {inferred_dtype}\")`\r\nthe problem would be solved\r\n\r\nDetails can be found in [here](https://github.com/pandas-dev/pandas/pull/59649/files)\n\n### Alternative Solutions\n\nUse a debugger and set a break point\n\n### Additional Context\n\n_No response_\n", "patch": "diff --git a/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py b/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py\nindex 3cb0e75cfb815..05e1a36877e06 100644\n--- a/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py\n+++ b/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py\n@@ -255,7 +255,9 @@ def _validate(data):\n         inferred_dtype = lib.infer_dtype(values, skipna=True)\n \n         if inferred_dtype not in allowed_types:\n-            raise AttributeError(\"Can only use .str accessor with string values!\")\n+            raise AttributeError(\n+                f\"Can only use .str accessor with string values, not {inferred_dtype}\"\n+            )\n         return inferred_dtype\n \n     def __getitem__(self, key):\n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 174399, "completion_tokens": 833}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\nexplore_graph_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 1, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Args:\n    start_entities:\n    direction:\n    traversal_depth:\n    entity_type_filter:\n    dependency_type_filter:\n    Returns:\n\nexplore_tree_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 2, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Analyzes and displays the dependency structure around specified entities in a code graph.\n    This function searches and presents relationships and dependencies for the specified entities (such as classes, functions, files, or directories) in a code graph.\n    It explores how the input entities relate to others, using defined types of dependencies, including 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes' and 'inherits'.\n    The search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n    Example Usage:\n    1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n    direction='downstream',\n    traversal_depth=2,\n    entity_type_filter=['class', 'function'],\n    dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n    2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n    direction='upstream',\n    traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n    Notes:\n    * Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n    * Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n    * Graph Context: The function operates on a pre-built code graph containing entities (e.g., files, classes and functions) and dependencies representing their interactions and relationships.\n    Parameters:\n    ----------\n    start_entities : list[str]\n    List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\n    - Entities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\"\n    (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\n    - For files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n    direction : str, optional\n    Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are:\n    - 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n    - 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others\n    (how others depend on them).\n    - 'both': Traversal in both directions.\n    Default is 'downstream'.\n    traversal_depth : int, optional\n    Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).\n    Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n    Default is 2.\n    entity_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all entity types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    dependency_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all dependency types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    Returns:\n    -------\n    result : object\n    An object representing the traversal results, which includes discovered entities and their dependencies.\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n\n- Comprehensive Dependency Analysis: Prioritize analyzing the code dependencies of functions, methods, or classes mentioned in the problem description or those identified in relevant locations. Use `search_invoke_and_reference` as the primary tool to ensure all interactions within the codebase are captured.\n\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many commands OR too much code in one \"execute\" block.\nInclude ONLY ONE <execute_ipython> per response, unless the assistant is finished with the task or needs more input or action from the user in order to proceed.\nIf the assistant is finished with the task you MUST include <finish></finish> in your response.\nIMPORTANT: Execute code using <execute_ipython> whenever possible.\nThe assistant should utilize full file paths to prevent path-related errors.\nThe assistant must avoid apologies and thanks in its responses.\nYou have access to the following functions:\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #1: finish ----\nDescription: Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task.\nNo parameters are required for this function.\n---- END FUNCTION #1 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #2: search_code_snippets ----\nDescription: Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n\nParameters:\n  (1) search_terms (array, optional): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (2) line_nums (array, optional): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (3) file_path_or_pattern (string, optional): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n---- END FUNCTION #2 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #3: get_entity_contents ----\nDescription: \nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n\nParameters:\n  (1) entity_names (array, required): A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n---- END FUNCTION #3 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #4: explore_tree_structure ----\nDescription: \nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n\nParameters:\n  (1) start_entities (array, required): List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n  (2) direction (string, optional): Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.\nAllowed values: [`upstream`, `downstream`, `both`]\n  (3) traversal_depth (integer, optional): Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n  (4) entity_type_filter (['array', 'null'], optional): List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.\n  (5) dependency_type_filter (['array', 'null'], optional): List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.\n---- END FUNCTION #4 ----\n\n\nIf you choose to call a function ONLY reply in the following format with NO suffix:\n\n<function=example_function_name>\n<parameter=example_parameter_1>value_1</parameter>\n<parameter=example_parameter_2>\nThis is the value for the second parameter\nthat can span\nmultiple lines\n</parameter>\n</function>\n\n<IMPORTANT>\nReminder:\n- Function calls MUST follow the specified format, start with <function= and end with </function>\n- Required parameters MUST be specified\n- Only call one function at a time\n- You may provide optional reasoning for your function call in natural language BEFORE the function call, but NOT after.\n- If there is no function call available, answer the question like normal with your current knowledge and do not tell the user about function calls\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "\nGiven the following GitHub issue description, your objective is to localize the specific files, classes or functions, and lines of code that are relevant to the solution for the issue. More specifically, these issues are all \"feature request\" issues, which means that you need to find the locations the feature described in the issue.\n\nFollow these steps to localize the issue:\n## Step 1: Categorize and Extract Key Problem Information\n - Classify the issue description into the following categories:\n    Requested feature description, behavior with out this feature, expected behavior with the feature, and additional context.\n - Identify modules in the 'pandas-dev' package mentioned in each category.\n - Use extracted keywords and line numbers to search for relevant code references for additional context.\n\n## Step 2: Locate Referenced Modules\n- Accurately determine specific modules\n    - Explore the repo to familiarize yourself with its structure.\n    - Analyze the described workflow to identify specific modules or components being referenced.\n- Pay special attention to distinguishing between modules with similar names using context and described workflow.\n- Output Format for collected relevant modules:\n    - Use the format: 'file_path:QualifiedName'\n    - E.g., for a function `calculate_sum` in the `MathUtils` class located in `src/helpers/math_helpers.py`, represent it as: 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'.\n\n## Step 3: Analyze the feature\n- Understand the Purpose of the Feature in the Context of the Codebase\n    - Understand what the feature is supposed to do.\n    - Understand why it is needed.\n    - Understand how it fits into the overall functionality of the codebase.\n- Analyze the Feature's Relationship with the Codebase\n    - Identify the key affected components of the feature.\n    - Understand how these components interact with each other and with existing code.\n    - Consider the inputs, outputs, and side effects of the feature.\n\n## Step 4: Locate Areas for Feature Implementation\n- Locate specific files, functions, or lines of code implementing the key part of the requested feature or containing critical information for resolving the issue.\n- Consider upstream and downstream dependencies that may affect or be affected by the issue.\n- Think Thoroughly: List multiple potential solutions and consider edge cases that could impact the resolution.\n\n## Output Format for Final Results:\nYour final output should list the locations requiring modification, wrapped with triple backticks ```\nEach location should include the file path, class name (if applicable), function name, or line numbers, ordered by importance.\nYour answer would better include about 5 files.\n\nIMPORTANT FORMAT RULES:\n- Output ONLY the location block, followed by `<finish></finish>`.\n- Do NOT include explanations, bullets, headings, numbering, summaries, or notes before or after the location block.\n- Each file path MUST appear on its own line and end with `.py`.\n- Do NOT write `file.py:QualifiedName` on a single line in the final answer.\n- After a file path line, use separate `line:`, `class:`, and `function:` lines as needed.\n\n### Examples:\n```\nfull_path1/file1.py\nline: 10\nclass: MyClass1\nfunction: my_function1\n\nfull_path2/file2.py\nline: 76\nfunction: MyClass2.my_function2\n\nfull_path3/file3.py\nline: 24\nline: 156\nfunction: my_function3\n```\n\nReturn just the location(s)\n\nNote: Your thinking should be thorough and so it's fine if it's very long.\n\n--- BEGIN PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\nTitle: ENH: increase verbosity on error\n\n### Feature Type\n\n- [ ] Adding new functionality to pandas\n\n- [X] Changing existing functionality in pandas\n\n- [ ] Removing existing functionality in pandas\n\n\n### Problem Description\n\nOn a specific type error the error causing type is not logged in the error\n\n### Feature Description\n\nBy replacing:\r\n`raise AttributeError(\"Can only use .str accessor with string values!\")`\r\nwith\r\n`raise AttributeError(f\"Can only use .str accessor with string values! Inferred dType: {inferred_dtype}\")`\r\nthe problem would be solved\r\n\r\nDetails can be found in [here](https://github.com/pandas-dev/pandas/pull/59649/files)\n\n### Alternative Solutions\n\nUse a debugger and set a break point\n\n### Additional Context\n\n_No response_\n--- END PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\n\nIMPORTANT: You should ONLY interact with the environment provided to you AND NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\nDon't include any lambda functions!\nYou should NOT modify any files!\n"}, {"content": "Let's start by searching for the specific line of code mentioned in the feature description.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=line_nums>[2232]</parameter>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>pandas/core/accessor.py</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"pandas/core/accessor.py:line 2232\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound code snippet in file `pandas/core/accesso