ult:\nFound directory `pandas/core/methods`.\nFound file `pandas/core/methods/__init__.py`.\nFound file `pandas/core/methods/describe.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Use more detailed query to get the full content of some if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"method=values\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/core/frame.py:DataFrame.values`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n13694 | def values(self) -> np.ndarray:\n13695 |         \"\"\"\n13696 |         Return a Numpy representation of the DataFrame.\n13697 | \n13698 |         .. warning::\n13699 | \n13700 |            We recommend using :meth:`DataFrame.to_numpy` instead.\n13701 | \n13702 |         Only the values in the DataFrame will be returned, the axes labels\n13703 |         will be removed.\n13704 | \n13705 |         Returns\n13706 |         -------\n13707 |         numpy.ndarray\n13708 |             The values of the DataFrame.\n13709 | \n13710 |         See Also\n13711 |         --------\n13712 |         DataFrame.to_numpy : Recommended alternative to this method.\n13713 |         DataFrame.index : Retrieve the index labels.\n13714 |         DataFrame.columns : Retrieving the column names.\n13715 | \n13716 |         Notes\n13717 |         -----\n13718 |         The dtype will be a lower-common-denominator dtype (implicit\n13719 |         upcasting); that is to say if the dtypes (even of numeric types)\n13720 |         are mixed, the one that accommodates all will be chosen. Use this\n13721 |         with care if you are not dealing with the blocks.\n13722 | \n13723 |         e.g. If the dtypes are float16 and float32, dtype will be upcast to\n13724 |         float32.  If dtypes are int32 and uint8, dtype will be upcast to\n13725 |         int32. By :func:`numpy.find_common_type` convention, mixing int64\n13726 |         and uint64 will result in a float64 dtype.\n13727 | \n13728 |         Examples\n13729 |         --------\n13730 |         A DataFrame where all columns are the same type (e.g., int64) results\n13731 |         in an array of the same type.\n13732 | \n13733 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n13734 |         ...     {\"age\": [3, 29], \"height\": [94, 170], \"weight\": [31, 115]}\n13735 |         ... )\n13736 |         >>> df\n13737 |            age  height  weight\n13738 |         0    3      94      31\n13739 |         1   29     170     115\n13740 |         >>> df.dtypes\n13741 |         age       int64\n13742 |         height    int64\n13743 |         weight    int64\n13744 |         dtype: object\n13745 |         >>> df.values\n13746 |         array([[  3,  94,  31],\n13747 |                [ 29, 170, 115]])\n13748 | \n13749 |         A DataFrame with mixed type columns(e.g., str/object, int64, float32)\n13750 |         results in an ndarray of the broadest type that accommodates these\n13751 |         mixed types (e.g., object).\n13752 | \n13753 |         >>> df2 = pd.DataFrame(\n13754 |         ...     [\n13755 |         ...         (\"parrot\", 24.0, \"second\"),\n13756 |         ...         (\"lion\", 80.5, 1),\n13757 |         ...         (\"monkey\", np.nan, None),\n13758 |         ...     ],\n13759 |         ...     columns=(\"name\", \"max_speed\", \"rank\"),\n13760 |         ... )\n13761 |         >>> df2.dtypes\n13762 |         name          object\n13763 |         max_speed    float64\n13764 |         rank          object\n13765 |         dtype: object\n13766 |         >>> df2.values\n13767 |         array([['parrot', 24.0, 'second'],\n13768 |                ['lion', 80.5, 1],\n13769 |                ['monkey', nan, None]], dtype=object)\n13770 |         \"\"\"\n13771 |         return self._mgr.as_array()\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"method=time method=index method=values\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `asv_bench/benchmarks/strings.py:Methods`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nJust show the structure of this class due to respon