0 |             Number of decimal places to round to. If decimals is negative,\n2471 |             it specifies the number of positions to the left of the decimal point.\n2472 |         *args, **kwargs\n2473 |             Additional arguments and keywords have no effect but might be\n2474 |             accepted for compatibility with NumPy.\n2475 | \n2476 |         Returns\n2477 |         -------\n2478 |         Series\n2479 |             Rounded values of the Series.\n2480 | \n2481 |         See Also\n2482 |         --------\n2483 |         numpy.around : Round values of an np.array.\n2484 |         DataFrame.round : Round values of a DataFrame.\n2485 | \n2486 |         Notes\n2487 |         -----\n2488 |         For values exactly halfway between rounded decimal values, pandas rounds\n2489 |         to the nearest even value (e.g. -0.5 and 0.5 round to 0.0, 1.5 and 2.5\n2490 |         round to 2.0, etc.).\n2491 | \n2492 |         Examples\n2493 |         --------\n2494 |         >>> s = pd.Series([-0.5, 0.1, 2.5, 1.3, 2.7])\n2495 |         >>> s.round()\n2496 |         0   -0.0\n2497 |         1    0.0\n2498 |         2    2.0\n2499 |         3    1.0\n2500 |         4    3.0\n2501 |         dtype: float64\n2502 |         \"\"\"\n2503 |         nv.validate_round(args, kwargs)\n2504 |         new_mgr = self._mgr.round(decimals=decimals)\n2505 |         return self._constructor_from_mgr(new_mgr, axes=new_mgr.axes).__finalize__(\n2506 |             self, method=\"round\"\n2507 |         )\n```\n\n\n##Searching for entity `pandas/core/indexes/accessors.py:CombinedDatetimelikeProperties`...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `pandas/core/indexes/accessors.py:CombinedDatetimelikeProperties`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `pandas/core/indexes/accessors.py:CombinedDatetimelikeProperties`.\n```\n602 | class CombinedDatetimelikeProperties(\n603 |     DatetimeProperties, TimedeltaProperties, PeriodProperties\n604 | ):\n605 |     \"\"\"\n606 |     Accessor object for Series values' datetime-like, timedelta and period properties.\n607 | \n608 |     See Also\n609 |     --------\n610 |     DatetimeIndex : Index of datetime64 data.\n611 | \n612 |     Examples\n613 |     --------\n614 |     >>> dates = pd.Series(\n615 |     ...     [\"2024-01-01\", \"2024-01-15\", \"2024-02-5\"], dtype=\"datetime64[ns]\"\n616 |     ... )\n617 |     >>> dates.dt.day\n618 |     0     1\n619 |     1    15\n620 |     2     5\n621 |     dtype: int32\n622 |     >>> dates.dt.month\n623 |     0    1\n624 |     1    1\n625 |     2    2\n626 |     dtype: int32\n627 | \n628 |     >>> dates = pd.Series(\n629 |     ...     [\"2024-01-01\", \"2024-01-15\", \"2024-02-5\"], dtype=\"datetime64[ns, UTC]\"\n630 |     ... )\n631 |     >>> dates.dt.day\n632 |     0     1\n633 |     1    15\n634 |     2     5\n635 |     dtype: int32\n636 |     >>> dates.dt.month\n637 |     0    1\n638 |     1    1\n639 |     2    2\n640 |     dtype: int32\n641 |     \"\"\"\n642 | \n643 |     def __new__(cls, data: Series):  # pyright: ignore[reportInconsistentConstructor]\n644 |         # CombinedDatetimelikeProperties isn't really instantiated. Instead\n645 |         # we need to choose which parent (datetime or timedelta) is\n646 |         # appropriate. Since we're checking the dtypes anyway, we'll just\n647 |         # do all the validation here.\n648 | \n649 |         if not isinstance(data, ABCSeries):\n650 |             raise TypeError(\n651 |                 f\"cannot convert an object of type {type(data)} to a datetimelike index\"\n652 |             )\n653 | \n654 |         orig = data if isinstance(data.dtype, CategoricalDtype) else None\n655 |         if orig is not None:\n656 |             data = data._constructor(\n657 |                 orig.array,\n658 |                 name=orig.name,\n659 |                 copy=False,\n660 |                 dtype=orig._values.categories.dtype,\n661 |                 index=orig.index,\n662 |             )\n663 | \n664 |         if isinstance(data.dtype, ArrowDtype) and data.dtype.kind in \"Mm\":\n665 |             return ArrowTemporalProperties(data, orig)\n666 |         if lib.is_np_dtype(data.dtype, \"M\"):\n667 |             return DatetimeProperties(data, orig)\n668 |         elif isinstance(data.dtype, DatetimeTZDtype):\n669 |             return DatetimeProperties(data, orig)\n670 |         elif lib.is_np_dtype(data.dtype, \"m\"):\n671 |             return TimedeltaProperties(data, orig)\n672 |         elif isinstance(data.dtype, PeriodDtype):\n673 |             return PeriodProperties(data, orig)\n674 | \n675 |         raise AttributeError(\"Can only use .dt accessor with datetimelike values\")\n```\n"}, {"content": "Based on the search results, we need to modify the documentation for the `Series.round` method to include `Series.dt.round` in the \"See also\" section. Additionally, we should examine the `CombinedDatetimelikeProperties` class and its associated properties to understand how `Series.dt.round` might be implemented or documented.\n\nLet's focus on the relevant documentation files for these methods. We will search for the `Series.round` documentation file and then check if there are any files related to `Series.dt` in the documentation directory.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>\n[\"Series.round\"]\n</parameter>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>\n\"doc/source/reference/api/pandas.Series.round.rst\"\n</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nNo files found for file pattern '\"doc/source/reference/api/pandas.Series.round.rst\"'. Will search all files.\n...\n##Searching for term \"Series.round\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/core/series.py:Series.round`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `round`.\n```\n2463 | def round(self, decimals: int = 0, *args, **kwargs) -> Series:\n2464 |         \"\"\"\n246